{"type": "say", "text": "Elena, here is my formal credit risk condition. The single-issuer limit is $10 million, and we already have $4 million of exposure to this issuer. That leaves only $6 million of capacity before we breach the limit. A full $12 million face purchase — roughly $9.84 million at 82 cents — would put us at approximately $13.84 million total exposure, well above the limit. My formal condition is that any purchase must be sized so that total exposure to this issuer, including the existing $4 million position, does not exceed $10 million. That means the maximum additional exposure we can take on is $6 million. I'd also note that Hassan's liquidity constraint of $5 million over four trading days is actually tighter than the $6 million credit limit headroom, so the binding constraint on size may come from the markets side. From a credit risk standpoint, a partial purchase sized to stay within the $10 million single-issuer limit is acceptable, provided all other workstream conditions are also met."}
